---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const maslow = [
  { level: 'Self-actualisation', eg: 'Challenging projects, training', w: 40 },
  { level: 'Esteem', eg: 'Promotion, titles, praise', w: 55 },
  { level: 'Love and belonging', eg: 'Team events, friendly culture', w: 70 },
  { level: 'Safety', eg: 'Permanent contract, safe workplace', w: 85 },
  { level: 'Physiological', eg: 'A wage for food and rent', w: 100 },
];
---
<Layout title="2.4 Motivation Theories">
  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/ibbm/">Home</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <a href="/ibbm/unit2/">Unit 2</a><span class="sep">›</span>
    <span>2.4 Motivation Theories</span>
  </div>
  <div class="topic-hero">
    <span class="topic-number">2.4</span>
    <h1>Motivation Theories</h1>
    <p class="subtitle">Taylor, Maslow and Herzberg: why people work hard, and what managers can do about it. HL: McClelland, Deci and Ryan, Adams and Vroom.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="tabs">
    <button class="tab-btn active" onclick="switchTab(this,'notes')">Notes</button>
    <button class="tab-btn" onclick="switchTab(this,'practice')">Practice</button>
  </div>

  <!-- NOTES -->
  <div id="notes" class="tab-content active">

    <div class="learning-goals">
      <div class="learning-goals-title">Learning Goals</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Define intrinsic and extrinsic motivation</li>
        <li>Outline Taylor, Maslow and Herzberg</li>
        <li>Apply each theory to a business, and evaluate it</li>
        <li>Recommend a theory for a manager, and justify it</li>
        <li>Explain financial and non-financial rewards, and types of training</li>
        <li>Outline, apply and evaluate McClelland, Deci and Ryan, Adams and Vroom <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
        <li>Explain one situation using more than one theory <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
        <li>Labour turnover, appraisal, recruitment <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Intrinsic vs extrinsic</h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Intrinsic</div>
          <p>From <strong>inside</strong> a person. Internal rewards: fun, pleasure, meaning.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Extrinsic</div>
          <p>From <strong>outside</strong> a person. External rewards: money, points, praise, threats.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Taylor: scientific management (1911)</h2>
      <p>American engineer in steel mills, where workers set their own pace. He saw two problems: workers did less than they could, and even at full effort output was low. His answer: <strong>study work scientifically, and pay for results</strong>. He timed every task with a stopwatch to cut waste and find the fastest way.</p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem; color:var(--text-muted);">Context: mass factory production had arisen within a generation or two. Mechanise production... mechanise people!</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Efficiency</th><th>Motivation</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>One specialised task each. Managers time and analyse it. Workers trained in that task only. No innovating.</td>
            <td>Base rate of pay, paid if a minimum target is met, plus a bonus per unit above target. Money is the motivator.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Economic man: carrot and stick</div>
        <p>Pay is the main motivator. <strong>Piece rate</strong>: pay per unit. Managers enforce targets. Still used in factories and in commission-based jobs.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Workers become expert at one task. Standard products, less waste. Higher pay possible.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Boring, so demotivating. No worker ideas. Modern jobs need creativity. Ignores other motivation factors.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Maslow: hierarchy of needs (1943)</h2>
      <p>Psychologist Abraham Maslow said needs come in a set order: meet a lower need first. (The pyramid was added later by others.)</p>
      <div style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; align-items:center; gap:4px; margin:1rem 0;">
        {maslow.map((m, i) => (
          <div style={`width:${m.w}%; background:var(--blue-pale); border:1px solid var(--blue); border-radius:var(--radius); padding:0.4rem 0.6rem; text-align:center; font-size:0.85rem;`}>
            <strong>{5 - i}. {m.level}</strong><br /><span style="color:var(--text-muted);">{m.eg}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">The needs themselves: physiological (air, food, water, sleep); safety (security, safe work, savings); love and belonging (friends, team); esteem (achievement, respect, status); self-actualisation (reaching your full potential).</p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Simple to apply (?). Shows pay is not the only need.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>The order is arguably not fixed. Differs across cultures. Hard to measure. Meeting the same need in different people takes different actions by the company.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Herzberg: two-factor theory (1959)</h2>
      <p>Frederick Herzberg interviewed about 200 accountants and engineers. He found satisfaction and dissatisfaction have <strong>different causes</strong>: motivators drive satisfaction, hygiene factors drive dissatisfaction. <strong>Fix hygiene factors first, then add motivators.</strong></p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Hygiene factors</th><th>Motivators</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Company policy, supervision, relationships, working conditions, salary, job security</td>
            <td>Achievement, recognition, the work itself, responsibility, advancement, growth</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Prevent dissatisfaction. Fixing them leaves staff neutral, not motivated</td>
            <td>Create satisfaction</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Simple and practical. Supports job enrichment.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Small, narrow sample. Self-reported. Salary can motivate some people.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Taylor vs Herzberg</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Taylor</th><th>Herzberg</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Pay</strong></td><td>Motivates</td><td>Only prevents dissatisfaction</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>The job</strong></td><td>Narrow, repeated tasks</td><td>Responsibility and growth</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Suits</strong></td><td>Repetitive work</td><td>Skilled work</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Where this is assessed (SL)</div>
        <p>Paper 1: case study questions, including a 10-mark extended response. Paper 2 Section B: structured questions then a 10-mark extended response, possibly as part of a more numbers-based question. IA: possible if your research question is about staff motivation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Financial and non-financial rewards</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Financial reward</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Salary</strong></td><td>Fixed annual pay, paid monthly</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Wages</strong></td><td>Time rate: pay per hour worked. Piece rate: pay per unit made (Taylor)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Commission</strong></td><td>A percentage of the value of sales made</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Performance-related pay (PRP)</strong></td><td>Extra pay for meeting individual targets, often set at appraisal</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Profit-related pay</strong></td><td>A share of the business's profit paid to staff</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Employee share ownership</strong></td><td>Staff are given or sold shares, so they gain when the business does</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Fringe payments</strong></td><td>Perks on top of pay: company car, health insurance, discounts</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Non-financial reward</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Job enrichment</strong></td><td>More challenging, responsible work (Herzberg's motivators)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Job rotation</strong></td><td>Moving between tasks to reduce boredom</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Job enlargement</strong></td><td>More tasks at the same level</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Empowerment</strong></td><td>Freedom to make decisions about your own work</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Purpose</strong></td><td>The chance to make a difference</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Teamwork</strong></td><td>Working in groups: belonging (Maslow)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p><strong>Training:</strong> <strong>induction</strong> (introducing new staff to the business), <strong>on the job</strong> (learning while working, e.g. shadowing), <strong>off the job</strong> (courses away from the workplace).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Two kinds of theory <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Need-based: what do people need?</div>
          <p>Maslow, Herzberg, McClelland, Deci and Ryan</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Process-based: how do people think about work, and what do they expect?</div>
          <p>Adams (equity), Vroom (expectancy)</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout exam-tip">
        <div class="callout-title">Where this is assessed (HL)</div>
        <p>Paper 2: structured questions and the 10-mark extended response. Paper 3: HL content can appear in the stimulus questions. Paper 1: SL content only, so these theories do not appear. IA: possible if your research is about staff motivation.</p>
      </div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Command term</th><th>AO</th><th>Marks</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Outline</td><td>AO1</td><td>2</td><td>Outline one part of Vroom's expectancy theory.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Explain, apply</td><td>AO2</td><td>2 to 4</td><td>Explain how equity theory could apply to the business.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Analyse</td><td>AO2</td><td>4 to 6</td><td>Analyse the staff's motivation using McClelland's theory.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Evaluate, discuss, to what extent</td><td>AO3</td><td>10</td><td>To what extent is self-determination theory useful to the business?</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">10-mark answers: apply the theory to the case, weigh both sides, and justify a conclusion. Marks shown are typical.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">McClelland: acquired needs (1961) <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <p><em>The Achieving Society.</em> Everyone has all three needs, but weighs them differently. Culture affects the mix. Managers can change the job to fit the person.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Wants</th><th>Risk</th><th>What a manager does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Achievement</strong></td><td>Challenge, mastering skills, feedback</td><td>Bored if tasks do not develop them</td><td>Stretch tasks and constructive feedback</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Power</strong></td><td>Influence, teaching or mentoring others</td><td>Uses power at others' expense</td><td>Mentoring roles, and watch the effect on others</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Affiliation</strong></td><td>Strong relationships, teamwork</td><td>Weak as a manager, risk averse</td><td>Teamwork. Promotion may not be the best reward</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Explains why people differ. Shows how to match jobs to people.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Hard to measure each mix. Needs may change. Manager must know each person.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Deci and Ryan: self-determination (1985) <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <p><em>Intrinsic Motivation and Self-determination in Human Behaviour.</em> Aim: explain very high-quality motivation. The umbrella is <strong>flourishing</strong>, professionally and personally, through work. We flourish when three needs are met.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>At work</th><th>A manager could...</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Autonomy</strong></td><td>Control over what, when, how and with whom you work</td><td>Set the objective, let the team choose the route</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Competence</strong></td><td>Feeling capable: challenged, with the skills to succeed</td><td>Stretch with new tasks, give feedback</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Relatedness</strong></td><td>Belonging, with support professionally and personally</td><td>Teamwork, mentoring, chances to socialise</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Do bonuses really backfire?</div>
        <p>Not always. The research is mixed. Expected rewards for interesting tasks can reduce intrinsic motivation. Rewards that feel like control do more harm than rewards that give feedback. Dull, routine tasks have little intrinsic motivation to lose. Praise on progress can support competence. Researchers still debate how big the effect is.</p>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Needs work together.</strong> All three matter; one does not replace another. Autonomy is about choice, not working alone. Competence also grows by fitting in with others: support and feedback from colleagues. No relatedness can easily lead to demotivation. Autonomy is a strong motivator for experienced entrepreneurs. Does it matter to everyone?</p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Autonomy can raise motivation and productivity without a pay rise, so it can cost less than a bonus. Explains why some bonuses backfire, so managers should check how a reward feels. Links work to wellbeing, so it may reduce stress and turnover.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Hard to measure the needs. Autonomy may be valued differently across cultures. Pay still matters.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout remember">
        <div class="callout-title">Remembering the difference</div>
        <p>Acquired needs: I need my <strong>PAA</strong> (Power, Achievement, Affiliation).<br />Self-determination: I self-determine where I'm going by driving my <strong>CAR</strong> (Competence, Autonomy, Relatedness).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Adams: equity theory (1963) <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <p>John Stacey Adams. Motivation depends on <strong>perceived fairness</strong>: a ratio of inputs to outcomes. Perceptions can change when new information appears.</p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Inputs:</strong> time, effort, expertise, loyalty. <strong>Outcomes:</strong> pay, recognition, sense of achievement.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Equity norm</strong></td><td>Workers expect fair reward for their contribution</td><td>I work 50 hours, so I expect pay to match</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Social comparison</strong></td><td>Fairness is judged against peers' inputs and outcomes</td><td>A colleague with less experience earns more</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Cognitive distortion</strong></td><td>Under-rewarded workers change how they see inputs and outcomes, and may withdraw effort</td><td>That task was not really my job, so I will do less</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">Perceived inequity: the worker may...</div>
          <p>Reduce effort, withdraw goodwill, ask for a raise, compare with someone else, leave.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="content-card">
          <div class="content-card-title">The business faces...</div>
          <p>Lower output and quality, conflict and low morale, higher labour turnover, recruitment and training costs.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout key-term">
        <div class="callout-title">Real life: new hires paid more</div>
        <p><strong>Pay compression:</strong> new hires can earn more than long-serving staff. A Blind poll (Dec 2021) of 2,756 verified professionals: <strong>49%</strong> would quit or switch teams if a new graduate earned more than them. An Amazon example: $180,000 v a reported $300,000 for an external hire in the same role. How reliable is a poll of people who chose to answer?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Explains why pay gaps hurt motivation. Supports fair pay (gender gaps, executive pay). Shows non-pay fairness matters.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Fairness is subjective. Some people are more sensitive. Culture: equal pay may seem fair in one culture, pay for performance in another. Age, gender and personality change who we compare with.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Vroom: expectancy theory (1964) <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <p>Victor Vroom. Behaviour is motivated by <strong>expected results</strong>: people work hard if they think performance will be recognised and rewarded. A process theory: how motivation forms.</p>
      <div class="formula-box">Motivation = Expectancy × Instrumentality × Valence</div>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Simple question</th><th>Zero means</th><th>Manager raises it with...</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Expectancy</strong></td><td>Can I succeed?</td><td>I cannot do it</td><td>Realistic targets, training, resources</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Instrumentality</strong></td><td>Will I be rewarded if I succeed?</td><td>Nobody notices or pays</td><td>A clear link between performance and reward</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Valence</strong></td><td>Do I care about the reward?</td><td>I do not want it</td><td>Ask what people value. Culture matters</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Why multiply?</strong> If any part is 0, motivation is 0. 0 × 5 × 5: a target I cannot reach. 5 × 0 × 5: nobody links my results to a reward. 5 × 5 × 0: a prize I do not want. One weak link breaks the chain.</p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="callout exam-tip"><div class="callout-title">Strengths</div><p>Explains why a bonus can fail. Three checks for managers. Allows for different values.</p></div>
        <div class="callout warning"><div class="callout-title">Limits</div><p>Assumes people calculate rationally. Hard to measure the three scores. Ignores emotion and habit.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">Theories are lenses <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <p>Not "which theory is best?" Each theory is a lens: a different way to see the same situation. A manager may prefer one lens, choose the lens by the situation, or <strong>combine lenses</strong> to understand a complex situation, then form a logical, organised plan.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Pending teacher review</div><p>This section was added from the syllabus to fill gaps in the lesson slides.</p></div>

    <div class="section">
      <h2 class="section-title">More HR tools <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></h2>
      <div class="formula-box">Labour turnover = (staff leaving in a year ÷ average number of staff) × 100</div>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">High turnover raises recruitment and training costs and can signal demotivation.</p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Appraisal:</strong> formative (ongoing feedback to improve), summative (a judgement at the end of a period), 360-degree (feedback from managers, peers and subordinates), self-appraisal (staff assess themselves).</p>
      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Recruitment:</strong> internal (promote or move existing staff: cheaper, known people, but leaves a gap and no new ideas) or external (adverts, agencies, job sites: new ideas and a wider pool, but slower, costlier and riskier).</p>
    </div>

    <div class="recap">
      <div class="recap-title">Recap</div>
      <ul>
        <li>Intrinsic motivation comes from inside; extrinsic from outside rewards</li>
        <li>Taylor: break work into timed tasks, pay for results; money motivates</li>
        <li>Maslow: five needs in order, lower ones first; pay isn't the only need</li>
        <li>Herzberg: hygiene factors stop dissatisfaction, motivators create satisfaction</li>
        <li>Rewards can be financial (pay, commission, shares, perks) or non-financial (enrichment, empowerment, teamwork)</li>
        <li>HL: McClelland: everyone has a mix of achievement, power and affiliation (PAA)</li>
        <li>HL: Deci and Ryan: we flourish with competence, autonomy and relatedness (CAR)</li>
        <li>HL: Adams: motivation depends on perceived fairness of inputs v outcomes, compared with others</li>
        <li>HL: Vroom: motivation = expectancy × instrumentality × valence; any zero means zero</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div><!-- /notes -->

  <!-- PRACTICE -->
  <div id="practice" class="tab-content">

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 1: Why do you work hard?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Finish each sentence, out loud with a partner or on paper.</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>The class I worked hardest in last year was... because...</li>
          <li>A job I would like now is... because...</li>
          <li>If I was the boss, I would get my staff to work hard by...</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 2: Intrinsic or extrinsic?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="sort-grid">
          <div class="sort-item">A pay rise<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Enjoying a challenge<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Intrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">A bonus for hitting a target<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Feeling proud of your work<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Intrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Praise from a manager<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Avoiding a warning letter<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic</span></button></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 3: Taylor concept checks</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Define scientific management and outline how time and motion study was used. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Breaking tasks into timed steps to find the most efficient method, then training workers in it.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how Taylor expected pay to motivate workers. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Base rate, minimum target, bonus per unit above it: more effort, more pay.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why Taylor gave managers and workers different roles. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Managers plan and analyse; workers only carry out the task.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two advantages of his approach for a business. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Higher output per worker, standard products, less waste.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two disadvantages, one for workers and one for the business. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Workers: boring, narrow skills. Business: no worker ideas, costly to set up.</span></button></li>
          <li>Is Taylor's theory intrinsic or extrinsic? Justify. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Extrinsic: the reward is pay, not the work itself.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 4: Where does Taylor fit?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">For each context: how does Taylor work here, how does he not, and how well does he fit (1 to 5)? Use task breakdown, time and motion study, close supervision, piece rates.</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>A Victorian factory</li>
          <li>A fast food outlet</li>
          <li>A services office, e.g. an accounting firm: staff advise clients and solve a different problem each day</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answers</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Victorian factory: repetitive tasks, easy to time and pay per unit. Strong fit (4 to 5).<br />
            Fast food: standard tasks and timed steps work well; but service and staff turnover suffer from boring work. Good fit (3 to 4).<br />
            Services office: every problem is different, output is hard to count, creativity matters. Poor fit (1 to 2).
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These possible answers are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 5: To what extent?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">"Vietnam's culture is ideal for scientific management." (A major IB textbook.) To what extent do you agree?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 6: Maslow concept checks</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>List the five levels in order, with one workplace example of each. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Physiological (wage for food), safety (contract), belonging (team events), esteem (promotion), self-actualisation (challenging projects).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why Maslow thought lower needs must be met first. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Unmet lower needs dominate attention, so higher rewards do not motivate yet.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two ways a business could meet esteem needs. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Public recognition and praise; promotion or a job title.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how one reward (a pay rise) could meet more than one level. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A pay rise can buy security (safety) and signal status (esteem).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two limitations of Maslow's theory. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">The order is not fixed; needs differ across people and cultures; hard to measure.</span></button></li>
          <li>What does Maslow add that Taylor misses? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Non-pay needs such as belonging and growth. Taylor mainly sees money.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 7: What would Maslow say?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Which level(s) are affected, and what would Maslow predict or advise?</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Redundancies: how will the remaining staff react?</li>
          <li>A talented employee refuses a promotion to a desk job</li>
          <li>Tight budget: cancel the staff party?</li>
          <li>An education scheme for the lowest-paid staff?</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answers</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Redundancies: safety. Remaining staff feel insecure, so higher needs stop motivating. Reassure and communicate.<br />
            Refused promotion: the job may already meet self-actualisation, and a title (esteem) isn't what they want. Shows the order isn't fixed for everyone.<br />
            Staff party: belonging. Cutting it may hurt team spirit; find a cheaper way to keep it.<br />
            Education scheme: self-actualisation, but if pay barely covers physiological and safety needs, Maslow predicts it won't motivate much yet.
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These possible answers are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 8: Hygiene or motivator?</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="sort-grid">
          <div class="sort-item">Salary<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Achievement<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Company policy<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Recognition<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Working conditions<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Responsibility<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Supervision<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene</span></button></div>
          <div class="sort-item">Growth<br /><button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Motivator</span></button></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 9: Herzberg concept checks</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Define hygiene factor and motivator, with two examples of each. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene prevents dissatisfaction (salary, conditions). Motivator creates satisfaction (achievement, recognition).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why fixing hygiene factors does not motivate staff. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Removing dissatisfaction leaves staff neutral, not motivated.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain what a manager should do first and second under Herzberg, and why. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">First fix hygiene (pay, conditions), then add motivators (responsibility, growth).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why salary is a hygiene factor, and how this differs from Taylor. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Salary only prevents complaints, but Taylor treats pay as the main motivator.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two limitations of Herzberg's research. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Small sample of accountants and engineers; self-reports can be biased.</span></button></li>
          <li>Which theory best suits a software company? Justify. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Herzberg or Maslow, as staff want growth and autonomy; Taylor fits repetitive work.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 10: Southern Parcel Depot</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Southern Parcel Depot sorts parcels in Ho Chi Minh City. Staff earn a fair base wage plus a bonus for every parcel above target, and work in clean rooms on permanent contracts. Output is high, but a third of staff leave within a year, and exit surveys say work feels boring and machine-like. Three managers disagree. Ha wants bigger bonuses. Mai wants more team events and security. Vy wants staff to take responsibility and suggest improvements. The owner can afford only one change.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">For Ha, Mai and Vy: which theory is behind the idea, what's the evidence for and against, and how well does it fit (1 to 5)? Finish: The owner should back ___ because ___. However, ___.</div>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Points to consider</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Ha (Taylor): bonuses already drive high output, but pay is already fair and staff still leave, so more money may not fix boredom.<br />
            Mai (Maslow: belonging, safety): contracts are already permanent, so safety looks met; team events may help belonging, but the surveys complain about the work, not loneliness.<br />
            Vy (Herzberg motivators; Maslow esteem and self-actualisation): hygiene factors (wage, clean rooms, contracts) look fixed already, and the complaint is boring, machine-like work, so responsibility targets the cause. However, it takes time and may not suit every task.
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These points are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 11: Exam practice</div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Explain one way a manager could use Herzberg's theory at Southern Parcel Depot. [4 marks]</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 12: Recall the SL theories <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">No notes.</div>
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Who believed pay was the main motivator? <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Taylor.</span></button></li>
          <li>List Maslow's five levels in order. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Physiological, safety, love and belonging, esteem, self-actualisation.</span></button></li>
          <li>Give two hygiene factors and two motivators. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hygiene: salary, conditions, policy. Motivators: achievement, recognition, responsibility.</span></button></li>
          <li>Define intrinsic and extrinsic motivation. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Intrinsic: from the activity itself. Extrinsic: from a reward outside it.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 13: For discussion <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Think of something that felt unfair at school. What did you do?</li>
          <li>Think of a time when you expected to fail. What happened to your effort?</li>
          <li>Think of a time when you worked hard and nobody noticed. How did it feel?</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 14: My motivation profile <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Draw three axes (achievement, power, affiliation). Score each need from 0 to 5, mark it, and join the points to make your shape. Circle your strongest need.</li>
          <li>At the tip of each axis, write two things a teacher could do for you. Circle the one at each tip that would work best.</li>
          <li>Does your highest need match your strongest circled idea? Explain one mismatch: why might a self-assessment and a theory disagree?</li>
          <li>Judge: how useful is McClelland to a manager who has just met you?</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 15: McClelland concept checks <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Outline the three needs, with one workplace example of each. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Achievement: a stretching project. Power: mentoring or leading. Affiliation: teamwork.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why an affiliation-driven employee might struggle as a manager. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">They may have to judge colleagues and have hard conversations, which risks relationships.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how to motivate an achievement-driven employee, and one risk if you do not. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Stretch them with new tasks and feedback. If not, they get bored and may leave.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain one risk of a power-driven employee, and how a manager could channel it. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Power can harm others. Channel it into mentoring and monitor the effect.</span></button></li>
          <li>Identify two similarities and two differences between McClelland and Maslow. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Same: both based on needs, both include belonging. Different: no fixed order, and each person has a different mix.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how culture could change which need is strongest. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A culture that values group harmony may raise affiliation.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 16: Need check-up <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Score autonomy, competence and relatedness (1 to 5) for: my school; a club or sports team (or one you know); this class. Add a 5-word reason to each score, and one change to raise the lowest. Judge: does a high score on all three mean you are motivated? What does it miss?</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 17: Deci and Ryan concept checks <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Define autonomy, competence and relatedness, with one workplace example of each. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Autonomy: choosing how to do a task. Competence: feeling skilled and challenged. Relatedness: belonging to a supportive team.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why a financial bonus can reduce motivation, and when it might not. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">A bonus is an outside reward and can replace the pleasure in the work itself. It may not if the task is dull, or the reward gives useful feedback.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how a manager could raise autonomy without losing control of results. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Set the objective, let the team choose the method, check results not every step.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how competence links to McClelland's achievement, and how it also depends on fitting in with others. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Both want challenge and feedback. Competence also grows through support from colleagues.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why all three needs must be met. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Missing one (e.g. autonomy) can weaken motivation even if the others are met.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain one limitation of the theory. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Hard to measure, and valued differently across cultures.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 18: Should salaries be public? <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">For each issue, give one point for public salaries, one against, and say which wins: fairness and equity; the link between performance and pay; privacy and conflict.</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Evidence: secret pay can weaken the link between performance and pay, lowering performance and raising turnover (Belogolovsky and Bamberger, 2014).</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Finish: Salaries should / should not be public because ___. However, ___.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 19: Equity concept checks <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Define inputs and outcomes, with two examples of each. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Inputs: time, effort, skill. Outcomes: pay, recognition, achievement.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how the equity norm differs from social comparison. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Equity norm: expecting fair reward. Social comparison: judging fairness against others.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two ways an under-rewarded worker might respond, and a cost of each to the business. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Cut effort (lower output) or leave (turnover and recruitment cost).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why two workers on the same pay can disagree about fairness. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Perceived inputs and the comparison person differ.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain one non-financial way to improve perceived equity. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Flexible working or compassionate leave.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how culture or background could change what counts as fair. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Some cultures see equal pay as fair, others see pay for performance as fair. Age, gender and personality change who we compare with.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 20: Vroom concept checks <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Define expectancy, instrumentality and valence in your own words. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Expectancy: can I succeed? Instrumentality: will success be rewarded? Valence: do I want the reward?</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain why motivation is zero if any one part is zero. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">The parts are multiplied, so one zero makes the total zero.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how a bonus could fail on each of the three parts. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Target out of reach (expectancy); unclear who gets it (instrumentality); too small or not wanted (valence).</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how secret pay could lower instrumentality. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Staff cannot see that better performance leads to better pay.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain two things a manager could do to raise expectancy. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Set realistic targets; give training and resources.</span></button></li>
          <li>Explain how culture could change valence. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Some cultures value individual bonuses, others value team recognition or time with family.</span></button></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These answers are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 21: Expectancy calculator <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Score E, I and V (0 to 5), multiply, and say which part to fix first.</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>A sales bonus for a target almost nobody reaches</li>
          <li>A most-improved prize, but the criteria are never explained</li>
          <li>A promotion offered to someone who loves their current team</li>
          <li>Your own example</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="reveal reveal-block" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-expanded="false">
          <span class="reveal-label">Possible answers</span>
          <span class="reveal-text">
            Sales bonus: expectancy near 0. Fix first: a realistic target.<br />
            Most-improved prize: instrumentality low. Fix first: explain the criteria.<br />
            Promotion: valence low. Fix first: ask what they value (McClelland: affiliation).
          </span>
        </div>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These possible answers are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 22: Which theory? <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">Name the theory and the part.</div>
        <ol style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>A colleague does the same job and earns more. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Adams: social comparison.</span></button></li>
          <li>My manager tells me how to do every step. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Deci and Ryan: autonomy.</span></button></li>
          <li>I never reach the target, so I stopped trying. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Vroom: expectancy.</span></button></li>
          <li>I love leading and influencing others. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">McClelland: power.</span></button></li>
          <li>Nobody explains who gets the bonus. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Vroom: instrumentality.</span></button></li>
          <li>I have stopped learning new things at work. <button type="button" class="reveal" aria-expanded="false"><span class="reveal-text">Deci and Ryan: competence (or McClelland: achievement).</span></button></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="callout remember"><div class="callout-title">Note</div><p>These answers are pending teacher review.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 23: Three key words each <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">From memory, write three key words for each: Taylor, Maslow, Herzberg, McClelland, Deci and Ryan, Adams, Vroom. Then compare with a partner.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 24: Find the links <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <div class="exercise-q">For each pair: what do they have in common, and one difference?</div>
        <ul style="font-size:0.9rem;">
          <li>Maslow: self-actualisation and Deci and Ryan: flourishing</li>
          <li>Herzberg: motivators and McClelland: achievement</li>
          <li>Adams: fairness and Vroom: instrumentality</li>
          <li>Your own pair</li>
        </ul>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Start here: is self-determination theory an expansion of self-actualisation? Does it assume the lower needs are met, or not need them?</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="exercises">
      <div class="exercises-title">Activity 25: Lotus Apps <span class="hl-badge">HL</span></div>
      <div class="exercise">
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Background.</strong> Lotus Apps is a mobile app company in Ho Chi Minh City with 45 staff. For its first five years, developers chose their own projects, ate lunch together and knew the founder, Quan. Few staff left, and its apps won regional awards. Two years ago Lotus signed a large bank contract. Quan hired an operations manager, Nam, to hit the deadlines.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>The changes.</strong> Nam introduced software that logs every developer's hours and tasks. Managers now assign the projects. A quarterly bonus goes to any developer who completes 95% of assigned tasks. To compete for talent, Lotus hired new developers on salaries about 15% higher than existing staff in the same role.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>Thao.</strong> Thao is a senior developer with six years at Lotus. She used to lead the small team behind its best-known app. Now she mostly completes bank tasks set by Nam. The tasks keep changing, so she has missed the bonus twice, although colleagues praise her code reviews. HR accidentally shared a salary spreadsheet. A developer with two years of experience earns more than she does. Since then she arrives at nine, leaves at five and no longer joins lunch.</p>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;"><strong>The results.</strong> The bank project is on schedule. But no new app has won an award, and 9 of the 45 staff resigned this year, including two of the most creative developers. Exit surveys: 'no say in my work', 'I never get the bonus', 'unfair pay', 'the team is not the same', 'I have stopped learning'. Nam says the system works because deadlines are met. Trang, the HR manager, says pay is the problem. Quan says the studio has lost its spirit.</p>
        <div class="exercise-q">Why is Thao demotivated? For each theory (Taylor, Maslow, Herzberg, McClelland, Deci and Ryan, equity and expectancy): give evidence from the case, how it explains Thao, and how convincing it is (1 to 5).</div>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;">Finish: The most convincing explanation is ___ because ___. However, ___ also helps explain it because ___.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div><!-- /practice -->

